      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d2e7acff-6037-5cb0-8de3-de50b918e565",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Yealand Redmayne, North West England, Lancashire, Kendal, south Cumbria, Westmorland, River Ribble\n- Systems: Lancaster Canal, Leeds and Liverpool Canal\n- Structures: two bridges, milestone\n- Topics: listed buildings\n\nFacts:\n- The Lancaster Canal was originally planned to run from Westhoughton in Lancashire to Kendal in south Cumbria, then in Westmorland.\n- The section of the Lancaster Canal around the crossing of the River Ribble was never completed.\n- Much of the southern end of the Lancaster Canal was leased to the Leeds and Liverpool Canal and is now generally considered part of it.\n- In the parish of Yealand Redmayne, the Lancaster Canal passes through the area.\n- In Yealand Redmayne, two bridges crossing the Lancaster Canal are listed buildings, and a milestone on the canal's towpath is also listed.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
